<BaseLayout title="EXP-002 · Local Windows Execution | Airflow Lab" description="Airflow orchestration with Windows as the execution environment.">
  <article class="article">
    <div class="breadcrumbs">AIRFLOW LAB / EXPERIMENTS / EXP-002</div>
    <div class="eyebrow" style="margin-top:16px">Architecture / Windows Execution</div>
    <h1>Run Airflow Orchestration with Windows as the Execution Environment</h1>
    <p>
      Objective: keep Airflow focused on orchestration while Python/SQL/ML workloads execute in the
      Windows environment <code>env_mortgage</code>. This experiment records the networking tests,
      the Windows Agent approach, and the offline deployment design.
    </p>

    <h2>01 · Initial architecture</h2>
    <p>
      The original design placed Airflow on a remote Docker server and the workload on Windows.
      The remote server was <code>10.1.6.45</code>; the Windows execution machine was
      <code>10.1.7.58</code>.
    </p>
    <pre><code>Airflow Server 10.1.6.45
        |
        | HTTP :8000
        v
Windows 10.1.7.58
  FastAPI Agent
        |
        v
env_mortgage -> Python / SQL / ML</code></pre>

    <h2>02 · Why Git still works while Agent HTTP fails</h2>
    <p>
      Git pull/push and Airflow-to-Windows execution are different network flows.
      Git uses outbound HTTPS (normally TCP 443) from the machine to GitHub.
      The Agent test required an inbound connection from <code>10.1.6.45</code> to
      <code>10.1.7.58:8000</code>. Corporate networks may allow outbound traffic while
      restricting inbound traffic between subnets.
    </p>

    <h2>03 · Windows Agent</h2>
    <p>
      A FastAPI Agent was built at <code>E:\airflow\app.py</code>. It exposes
      <code>/health</code>, <code>/jobs/run</code> and <code>/jobs/{job_id}</code>, keeps an allowlist
      of runnable jobs, and uses <code>sys.executable</code> so jobs run with the same Conda
      environment as the Agent.
    </p>
    <pre><code>E:\airflow\
├── app.py
├── jobs\
│   └── hello_job.py
└── logs\
    └── hello_job.log</code></pre>

    <h2>04 · Evidence that the Agent itself is healthy</h2>
    <p>Windows showed the service listening on all interfaces:</p>
    <pre><code>TCP  0.0.0.0:8000  0.0.0.0:0  LISTENING  31136</code></pre>

    <p>The health check from Windows to its LAN IP succeeded:</p>
    <pre><code>python -c "import urllib.request; print(urllib.request.urlopen('http://10.1.7.58:8000/health', timeout=5).read().decode())"

&#123;"status":"ok","host":"Windows Local",
 "python":"c:\\ProgramData\\Anaconda3\\envs\\env_mortgage\\python.exe",
 "python_version":"3.10.13 ..."&#125;</code></pre>

    <h2>05 · Network troubleshooting</h2>
    <p>
      A Windows inbound firewall rule was created for the Airflow server only:
    </p>
    <pre><code>New-NetFirewallRule \
  -DisplayName "Airflow Local Agent 8000" \
  -Direction Inbound \
  -Protocol TCP \
  -LocalPort 8000 \
  -RemoteAddress 10.1.6.45 \
  -Action Allow</code></pre>

    <p>
      Despite this, the Airflow server timed out when calling the endpoint:
    </p>
    <pre><code>curl --noproxy "*" --connect-timeout 5 http://10.1.7.58:8000/health

curl: (28) Connection timed out after 5000 milliseconds

nc -vz -w 5 10.1.7.58 8000
Ncat: TIMEOUT.</code></pre>

    <p>
      The Windows firewall profile check showed the profiles disabled, while the dedicated
      rule was enabled and allowed TCP/8000. Taken together with the timeout, the evidence
      points to a routing/network ACL restriction between <code>10.1.6.45</code> and
      <code>10.1.7.58</code>, rather than a FastAPI or Uvicorn problem.
    </p>

    <h2>06 · Preferred architecture</h2>
    <p>
      The preferred target is an all-in-one Windows development and execution machine.
      Airflow runs locally in Docker/Linux containers; Windows remains the execution host
      with the existing <code>env_mortgage</code> environment.
    </p>
    <pre><code>Windows 10.1.7.58
|
+-- Docker Desktop
|   +-- Airflow
|   +-- PostgreSQL
|   +-- Redis
|
+-- E:\airflow
|   +-- dags\
|   +-- jobs\
|   +-- sql\
|   +-- logs\
|   +-- agent\
|
+-- Conda
    +-- env_mortgage

Airflow container -> host.docker.internal:8000 -> Windows Agent
                                      |
                                      v
                                env_mortgage</code></pre>

    <h2>07 · Responsibilities</h2>
    <div class="two">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Airflow</h3>
        <p>
          Scheduling, DAG parsing, dependencies, retries, timeouts, orchestration, task state
          and monitoring.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Windows</h3>
        <p>
          Python, pandas, DB2/MSSQL access, SQL, models, files, business logic and actual
          execution inside <code>env_mortgage</code>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2>08 · Where code lives</h2>
    <p>
      Source-of-truth can remain in Git/GitHub on an Internet-connected development machine.
      The Airflow scheduler still needs access to DAG files, so DAG source must eventually be
      present under Airflow's <code>dags/</code> directory. Business logic does not need to be
      copied into the Airflow container when the DAG only invokes the Windows Agent.
    </p>
    <pre><code>airflow-lab/
├── dags/       # Airflow orchestration code
├── jobs/       # Windows execution code
├── sql/        # SQL assets
├── config/     # configuration templates
├── agent/      # FastAPI Windows Agent
└── docs/       # experiment notes</code></pre>

    <h2>09 · Offline Windows machine</h2>
    <p>
      The target Windows machine may have no Internet access. Docker images can therefore be
      prepared on an Internet-connected machine, exported with <code>docker save</code>, copied
      offline, and imported with <code>docker load</code>. The Conda execution environment can
      be packaged with <code>conda-pack</code>.
    </p>
    <pre><code># Connected machine
docker images
docker save -o airflow-images.tar &lt;images...&gt;

# Offline 10.1.7.58
docker load -i airflow-images.tar

# Package Python execution environment
conda pack -n env_mortgage -o env_mortgage.tar.gz</code></pre>

    <h2>10 · Two network patterns</h2>
    <p><strong>Direct Agent call:</strong> Airflow calls Windows directly. This is simplest when the network allows
      <code>10.1.6.45 → 10.1.7.58:8000</code>.</p>
    <p><strong>Polling / outbound Agent:</strong> Windows initiates the connection to the Airflow-side service,
      polls for work, executes locally, and reports status. This is the fallback when corporate policy
      blocks inbound connections to workstations.</p>

    <h2>11 · Lessons learned</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Git connectivity does not prove bidirectional connectivity between internal subnets.</li>
      <li>When Uvicorn listens on <code>0.0.0.0:8000</code> and local/LAN health checks work, focus on network path, not application code.</li>
      <li>Keep Airflow as an orchestration layer; keep business execution in the environment that actually has the required DB drivers and ML libraries.</li>
      <li>For an offline host, transfer Docker images and Python environments explicitly instead of expecting runtime Internet access.</li>
      <li>Do not expose arbitrary shell execution from the Agent; use a strict job allowlist and authentication token.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="callout">
      Next: <strong>EXP-003 — Local Docker Airflow</strong>. Consolidate the stack onto Windows
